{
  "meta": {
    "title": "ZIP code prefix (ZIP3) to state and metro, for the US Home Energy Planner",
    "generated": "2026-10-12",
    "script": "tools/us/build_zip3.py",
    "format": "zip3: { \"<first three digits>\": [state, metro, near, nearMiles] }. state = USPS code of the state with most of the prefix's population. metro = slug of the covered metro (src/data/us/metros/) whose CBSA holds most of the prefix's population, else null (the largest share may be another CBSA or no CBSA at all). near and nearMiles are set only when metro is null: the covered metro nearest to the prefix whose metropolitan area includes part of the same state, and the distance in miles between the two population-weighted centers (rounded to 5); both null when no covered metro is in that state.",
    "method": "Each 2020 ZCTA's population (ACS 2020-2024 5-year, table B01003) is split across the counties it touches in proportion to the land area of each part (the 2020 relationship file has no population by part), each county is placed in its July 2023 CBSA, and the results are summed by the first three digits of the ZCTA. Centers are population-weighted averages of ZCTA internal points. Covered metros are matched to CBSAs by the MSA title in each metro record. Prefixes with no ZCTA (for example PO-box-only or single-organization prefixes) are absent, as are Puerto Rico and the island areas.",
    "notes": [
      "A prefix is mapped as a whole, so a few ZIP codes near metro edges get their neighbors' metro.",
      "Connecticut's 2020 counties were replaced by planning regions in the 2023 delineation, so Connecticut ZCTA parts get no CBSA here (299 parts). None of the 26 covered metros includes Connecticut, so no result changes."
    ],
    "sources": [
